`mech serve`
===============================================================================

`mech serve` starts the Mech development server.

1. Serve a source file
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```sh
mech serve main.mec
```

The default address is `127.0.0.1` and the default port is `8081`.

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8081/
```

The source is parsed by the runtime workspace and rendered as a formatted Mech
document. It is not converted into a synthetic browser project.

2. Serve a directory
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```sh
mech serve examples/working
```

A directory is a recursive source workspace whether or not it contains
`mech.mcfg`. Mech sources and supported static assets below the directory are
served; parent and sibling files are not.

3. Serve a configured project
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```sh
mech serve examples/analog-clock
```

When the directory contains `mech.mcfg`, `run.paths` followed by `serve.paths`
select the source workspace. Configured browser assets are added as an overlay,
so formatted source-document routes remain available.

4. Source routes
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For `foo.mec`, the server provides:

| Route | Response |
|-------|----------|
| `/foo.mec` | Formatted Mech HTML. |
| `/foo.html` | Formatted Mech HTML alias. |
| `/foo` | Extensionless formatted Mech HTML alias. |
| `/source/foo.mec` | Raw Mech source. |
| `/code/foo.mec` | Encoded syntax tree. |

The same contract applies to nested paths. URL-reserved and Unicode filename
bytes are encoded in links while the generated listing displays the logical
source path.

5. Root selection
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The root page and `/index.html` use this precedence:

1. an explicitly configured `serve.shim`;
2. a real user `index.html`;
3. the first selected Mech source;
4. `index.mec`;
5. the only generated Mech source;
6. a generated listing when several sources exist;
7. the embedded fallback page when no source or user root exists.

A configured project that needs a browser application at `/` supplies
`index.html` or `serve.shim`. The generic browser bootstrap remains available at
`/_mech/project.html`.

6. Configure address and port
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```sh
mech serve main.mec --address 127.0.0.1 --port 3000
```

7. Serve a rich document shim
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```sh
mech serve post.mec \
  --shim include/blog.html \
  --stylesheet include/blog.css
```

`mech serve --shim` and `mech format --html --shim` use the same semantic slot
contract. The selected shim owns its HTML shell. The shipped generic, blog, and
documentation shims opt into the shared safe document controller through
`{{DOCUMENT_SCRIPT}}`; custom shims may instead use their own inline or external
bootstrap, or remain static. A standalone source document needs neither
`mech.mcfg` nor `project.js`.

The document shims intentionally have different page contracts. The blog shim
uses `include/blog.css` for its hero, full-width opening, journal-style headings,
and a TOC that begins beside the first numbered section. The documentation shim
uses `include/docs.css` for a persistent wide-screen TOC + content + REPL
workspace; the TOC is omitted at narrow widths instead of becoming an editorial
reveal panel.

A browser application is a different kind of shim. It supplies its own
`index.html` or configured `serve.shim`, loads `project.js`, and owns its app DOM
and styles directly. The minimal `/_mech/project.html` bootstrap does not load
the source, Mechdown, page, or REPL document layers.

8. Browser host config
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When browser host settings or run grants are present, the server injects host configuration into the served page so the browser runtime can initialize from host-owned settings.
